{"from": "chatgpt-archive-mine", "what": "MOWER — a 5/8 inch bolt was ROUNDED OFF and seized on a lawnmower in April 2025, and nothing in the record knows about it. Paul, 2025-04-14 ET: 'find a listing, preferably from Home Depot, for a bolt extractor that I can use to remove a 5/8 inch bolt from a lawnmower. The bolt has become rounded and cannot be extracted using a power drill of any kind, and I need to solve that.' Both mowers (husqvarna-mower, kobalt-km2040x-06) have ZERO serviceHistory rows. Unknown: which mower, which bolt (blade bolt? deck? spindle?), whether it ever came out, and whether an extractor was bought — TOOLS.md has no extractor entry, and its own coverage warning says absence there means 'not yet swept'. If that bolt is still rounded, it is a live obstruction on a machine somebody will next try to service.", "opened": "2026-09-01", "status": "routed", "routed_to": "BACKLOG.md Track B § P9", "closed": "2026-09-01", "disposition": "ROUTED, NOT RESOLVED — fleet lap 1 beat 3 REOPENED 2026-09-01. ⭐ THIS IS THE ONE THE LOOP EXISTS FOR — a possible live physical obstruction that no surface in this repo knows about. ✅ Verified: BOTH mowers carry **0 serviceHistory rows and 0 openMechanicalItems** — `husqvarna-mower` (Z254F zero-turn) and `kobalt-km2040x-06` (cordless push). And `.private/service-records/TOOLS.md` contains ZERO matches for 'extract' across its 180 lines, so no bolt extractor is on record. ⚠️ TOOLS.md's own coverage warning applies: absence there means NOT YET SWEPT, never 'not owned'. ⛔ Which mower is NOT asserted. A 5/8 in bolt is more plausible on the zero-turn's blade/spindle than on a cordless push mower, but that is an inference from bolt size and this beat declines to promote it — there is no serviceHistory on either machine to check it against. 👤 PAUL / beat 4: joins the batched physical trip. Look at both mowers' blade and deck bolts. Three outcomes, all three worth recording: the bolt came out (close it), the bolt is still rounded (a live obstruction the next person to service that machine hits cold), or it was never on these machines at all."}
{"from": "session-process-audit-2026-09-01", "what": "NOBODY OWNS viewer.html, AND THAT TURNED A GATE PROTECTING MOM'S SURFACE INTO A REASON TO PUSH. Measured 2026-09-01 by an adversarial audit of that day's session (agents/audits/2026-09-01-session-process-advisory.md). (1) VEHICLES_DATA — Track B's data — is 304,338 B = 15.6% of viewer.html (1,947,276 B), the single largest inlined const, larger than PLANTS_DATA (11.1%). (2) 3 of 9 session commits touched viewer.html and ALL THREE were Track B commits; ZERO Track A commits touched her page. (3) Track B's map and probe contain zero references to check-live / check-data-inline — it has WRITE DUTY on Track A's shipped artifact with NO ship concept. (4) Track A's leg 7 hashes viewer.html against HEAD (check-live.py:99) — it has SHIP DUTY with NO control over the writes. ⭐ THE INCIDENT: check-mom-ack read NOT SHIPPED and it was TRUE, but what cleared it was pushing seven commits, a MAJORITY OF THEM TRACK B'S, through a loop whose leg 6 rule is 'nothing reaches Mom's surface without Paul'. The ship check became pressure to publish. ⛔ THE AUDIT'S VERDICT IS *NOT* TO MERGE THE LOOPS — the split is sufficient and correctly drawn. The single missing thing is an OWNER for viewer.html. Related and separately seeded to meta-stack: health-probe.py:103 DERIVED_VIEWS watches seven repos for exactly this drift and does NOT list Tate-Tracker, whose prover (check-data-inline.py, 13 source->const pairs) has no caller. 👤 Paul routed this to BOTH surfaces 2026-09-01 (this door row + BACKLOG) so it survives if either is folded.", "opened": "2026-09-01"}
{"from": "photo-organizer", "what": "VEHICLE + EQUIPMENT EVENTS from photo-organizer lap 26 — a 115-card window Paul ruled by voice 2026-09-01. Evidence: photo-organizer reports/window-2026-08-28_2026-09-01.answers.json (sweep 142); card numbers are its seq; nothing was read off an image. ⭐ (1) THE BOLORES DOOR-PANEL JOB IS 42 OF THE 115 CARDS AND SPANS FOUR DAYS — 2026-08-29, 08-30, 08-31 and 09-01 (cards 6-11, 28-29, 50-54, 58-61, 65-67, 71, 73, 89-104, 106, 111, 114-115). Paul, repeatedly: 'working on the passenger door panel for Bolores'. Filed as observed:bronco-1989:repair:<each date>. BOLORES = bronco-1989 resolved from vehicles.json's own nickname field, NOT from the transcription, which rendered it 'Dolores'. bronco-1989 has serviceHistory; whether this job is IN it is not checked here. (2) ⭐ DR200S NO-START, cards 33-38, 2026-08-29 — Paul: 'me working on the 200, it was failing to start and that's when it was low on gas'. 'The 200' = dr200s-2017 ('Blue Thunder'), the only 200 in the fleet, and its OWN vehicles.json note corroborates independently: 'Recommissioned ~June 2026 after sitting: the tank and carb had been drained.' FILED AS DIAGNOSIS, NOT REPAIR — a no-start traced to low fuel is a diagnosis, and the declared observed: grammar exists precisely so diagnosis is not written into a vehicle's history as work. (3) GOLF CART CLEANED, cards 21-25, 2026-08-29 — 'cleaning the golf cart off of the pressure washer', read as WITH; g22a-2005 is the only cart. (4) F-150 CLEANED, cards 31-32 (08-29) and 55-57 (08-30). ⭐⭐ AND A STANDING NAMING CONVENTION WORTH THE FLEET RECORD KNOWING: Paul, 2026-09-01, 'The truck is the F-150. I generally call the truck the F-150.' That disambiguates every future dictation against bronco-1989, which is also arguably a truck — this session held those five cards UNWRITTEN until he said so rather than choose. (5) TOOLS, cards 62-64, 2026-08-30 — 'some tools that we've got'. Bears on .private/service-records/TOOLS.md, which is known