{"from": "photo-organizer", "what": "FOLLOW-UP to the Bolores ask filed earlier today — a POINTER TO THE TRANSCRIPT, plus the two corrections that ask now needs. [paul-stated 2026-09-01: 'if we're seeding some of the photo results to Fernwood, providing an update to the repository and cycle responsible for Bolores, it should include a link to the detailed transcript, or at least the relevant text part of the transcript.'] THE TRANSCRIPT: photo-organizer/reports/bolores-restoration-narrative.md — regenerate any time with python3 tools/bolores_narrative.py. It is DERIVED from the answers files, never typed: 331 rulings, 107 episodes, 2025-09-26 to 2026-03-13, every quote Paul's own sentence stored in a paul_said field. ⭐ IT NOW ALSO CARRIES A REFINEMENTS SECTION — 168 photographs that ANOTHER LEDGER OWNS. Paul described them on the walk in more detail than the owning rows hold, and this walk deliberately did not overwrite those rows (an ingest reaching into another reviewer's judgments is the 2026-08-13 defect). Until tonight his extra detail was stranded in JSON that nothing rendered; he asked whether it was being captured, it was not, and it is now — recorded AGAINST the owning row, never as a competing claim. That is the half of this record a service history cannot hold: what he was doing and why, beside a row that only says which part it was.\n\nTHE RELEVANT TEXT, verbatim, so this loop need not open another repo — WHAT IS STILL OPEN:\n- **2025-11-26** #87 — “me trying to replace the front driver side door lock actuator, and seeing that it STILL DIDN'T WORK — so that's where this diagnosis issue comes from, from the front door lock actuator.”\n- **2026-02-23** #46 — “trying to figure out the door lock actuator voltage thing.”\n- **2026-03-11** #199 — “a picture of where the dash had BROKEN SCREW HOLES — what I need to do to fix that.”\n\n---\n\n⚠️ TWO CORRECTIONS TO THE EARLIER ASK, which said ~25 photographs of Bolores being DRIVEN were held unwritten and that a part ARRIVING had nowhere to go. BOTH ARE NOW FALSE and the earlier row should be read with this beside it. Paul declared two new kinds the same evening: `drive` ('we can have a vehicle being driven observation') released 24 photographs — the first drive to Tate, the drive home to Atlanta, the overlook, driving at night; and `part` ('we can do a part arriving category as well') released the RetroSound radio, which he clarified is 'specifically the radio for the Bronco, when that arrived in the mail to be installed in the Bronco'. ⭐ THAT PHOTOGRAPH TURNED OUT TO BE ONE THIS RECORD ALREADY HELD: IMG_5101.HEIC, 2026-01-27, already filed by the service-review ledger as observed:bronco-1989:part:2026-01-27 and sitting allowlisted as unverifiable because that ledger stores mappings, not prose. His sentence confirms it, months later, on the same image. ⭐ And observed:tiguan-2018:transport:2025-11-02 was RE-KINDED to drive at his instruction — the boat-hauling row. The grammar allowlist is now EMPTY. ⚠️ Paul also set the standing expectation: 'I'm fine if we continue to kinda morph and evolve these categories, I expect us to as we learn more.' So this vocabulary is not final and the fleet record should not treat a kind as permanent.", "opened": "2026-09-01", "why_here": "Carries the transcript link and the open items to the loop that owns bronco-1989, and corrects two claims in the ask filed earlier today that Paul's own rulings have since made false."}
{"from": "health-record", "opened": "2026-09-02", "why_here": "Two findings surfaced incidentally while opening the zone map from a health-record session on 2026-09-02. They belong to this loop, not that one, and would have been lost when that session cleared. Neither was acted on; both are reported, not fixed.", "what": "TWO INCIDENTAL FINDINGS ABOUT THE ZONE MAP, from an outside session that opened it and checked its provenance. (1) COLOR IS NOT IN THE RECORD, AND PAUL EXPECTED IT TO BE. All 23 zones in zones.json carry the IDENTICAL color [122, 149, 104] -- one olive green repeated 23 times, zero distinct values. Paul, on being shown the map: 'I've drawn them in full color before.' He is remembering something real, but it is not in canon: exports/fernwood-zones.kml GENERATES 23 distinct styles (ffb6f936, ff36f991, ...) from the zone id alone, and its own <description> says so verbatim -- 'Colors are generated for legibility and are NOT part of the record.' So the full color he remembers lives in a RENDERER (the KML palette, or viewer.html), never in zones.json. THE QUESTION FOR THIS LOOP, and it is a design question not a bug: SHOULD per-zone color be part of the record? Right now `type` varies across zones (planted / structure / turf) and survives every round trip, while color does not vary at all -- so any colored map is either deriving color from type or inventing it per-render, and two renderers can disagree about what a zone looks like with nothing to arbitrate. NOT ASSERTED: whether color was ever stored and lost, or never stored at all. The git history of zones.json would settle that in one command and was not run. (2) A SMALL VERIFIED BUG IN tools/zone-capture.py, one line. Any 404 crashes its request thread: log_message at line 85 does `if \"/api/save\" not in (args[0] if args else \"\")`, but on the send_error path args[0] is an HTTPStatus, not a string, so it raises TypeError: argument of type 'HTTPStatus' is not a container or iterable. MEASURED, not inferred -- driven 2026-09-02 by running the server, which threw a full traceback on GET /favicon.ico (the repo has no favicon.ico). IMPACT IS COSMETIC BUT NOT ZERO: every real request still returned 200 (/, /zones.json, the NAIP basemap all verified), so nothing is broken -- but the console prints an alarming multi-frame traceback during a capture session with Mom, which is exactly when nobody wants to wonder whether the tool just lost their work. Fix is to coerce args[0] to str before the membership test. ALSO WORTH KNOWING, not a finding: zones.json IS current and its provenance checks out. It was rebuilt 2026-09-01 21:44/22:38, NEWER than .private/zone-capture/areas-2026-08-30.json (18:34), and every apparent discrepancy against the 24 traced areas is documented and deliberate -- the retired golf vocabulary (Fairway->The Meadow, Meadow->The Turf, Fairway Border->The Green Ring), the 3 linear features deliberately held out of a schema that rebuilds {_meta, zones} wholesale, and Western Garden's own history log showing a 17-vertex retrace superseding the 24-vertex one. An outside reader diffing those two files WILL think canon is missing data; it is not, and _meta.fold_2026_08_31 explains all of it."}
{"from": "health-record", "opened": "2026-09-02", "why_here": "CROSS-LINK / partial withdrawal of the health-record row filed minutes earlier. That row was written without checking this repo's own commits from the same hour, and commit 44dd853 had already surveyed the same ground, better.", "what": "AMENDMENT TO THE health-record ROW FILED 2026-09-02 -- READ 44dd853 FIRST, IT OUTRANKS MINE. Commit 44dd853 'Zone consolidation SURVEYED' (2026-09-02 21:33, session 01E7iwDF3gJxRwR9nsKtBeHo) already answered the question my row raised, and answered it harder: exports/fernwood-zones.kml is IDENTICAL to canon -- 23 polygons, kml-to-zones.py dry run reports 'no geometry changes' -- so it was exported FROM canon and never edited, and PAUL'S RECENT DRAWS ARE NOT IN IT. If they exist they are in Google Earth Pro, unexported, or somewhere not yet found. That is the actual answer to his 'I've drawn them in full color before'; my row framed it as an open design question about whether color belongs in the record, which is a WEAKER framing of a question already narrowed to a location. WITHDRAW the framing, KEEP the datum: all 23 zones carry the identical color [122,149,104], and the KML's own <description> says its palette is generated and 'NOT part of the record'. WHAT MY ROW STILL ADDS, both untouched by 44dd853: (a) the one-line TypeError in tools/zone-capture.py log_message on any 404 path, measured by driving the server; (b) 44dd853 notes 'zone-capture.py ran 31x today means the local server was started, not that captures were written' -- CONFIRMED INDEPENDENTLY, one of those starts was mine at 21:52 from a health-record session, and it wrote nothing. THE LESSON, which is this inbox's business as much as the findings: I filed a row about a repo without reading that repo's commits from the same hour. Same failure Paul caught in health-record earlier today -- check what the source already holds before reporting a discovery to it. A cross-spine row is cheap to file and NOT free to process; an inbox that carries duplicates costs the receiving loop the time it was meant to save."}
{"from": "photo-organizer", "what": "A photographed VW service invoice in the photo library records a service event the Fernwood fleet record does not appear to hold — and its VIN, AS READ BY A MODEL, does not match the recorded Tiguan. Both halves need a human read.\n\nWHAT: photo-organizer uuid 83848F2F-2C60-46F3-BEAC-394AEC79A81E (IMG_1485.HEIC, taken 2022-06-06). A customer-copy invoice from Jim Ellis Volkswagen Atlanta, service date 02JUN22, stamped PAID, $331.42 — oil and filter change, tires rotated, all fluids topped, multi-point inspection. Odometer reads 62,148. A card slip in the same frame shows the same $331.42.\n\n⚠️ THE VALUES ARE A MODEL READ AND ARE UNVERIFIED. Per the standing rule, an OCR/vision read is a hypothesis until a deterministic source or Paul confirms it. Nothing here should reach vehicles.json on this basis alone.\n\nTHE DISCREPANCY, which is the actual reason this is being asked rather than filed: the VIN read off the page is 3VV3B7AX1LM••••••. The recorded tiguan-2018 carries 3VV1B7AX1JM (last six redacted). Those differ at two positions, including the 10th character — the model-year code — where the invoice reads L and the record reads J. So exactly one of three things is true: (a) the model misread the VIN, which is the most likely and is why this is flagged not filed; (b) the invoice is for a DIFFERENT Tiguan than the one on record; (c) the recorded VIN is wrong. A human looking at the photo settles it in seconds.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS EITHER WAY: service-records.manifest.json stages documents for gti-2016 and bronco-1989 only, so a 2022 Tiguan service event has no staged evidence regardless of which VIN is right. If the vehicle is on record and the service is not, that is a gap in the fleet's service history with a photograph attached.\n\nEVIDENCE PATH: the preview is at photo-organizer/exports/probe-previews/83848F2F-2C60-46F3-BEAC-394AEC79A81E_preview.jpeg. Pointer, not payload.", "opened": "2026-09-03"}
{"from": "session-2026-09-04-bolores-driver-door-washer", "opened": "2026-09-04", "why_here": "FIELD RESULT that CORRECTS a doctrine line already written into guides/bolores-door-panel-repair.md STEP 7. Filed at the door rather than folded, per Paul: 'save this as a seed for Fernwood to be incorporated at a later time.' Nothing in this row has been written to vehicles.json or the guide.", "what": "DRIVER-SIDE GLUED WASHER CAME OFF — and the outcome REFUTES this session's own prediction, which is the useful part.\n\nWHAT HAPPENED (Paul-reported, bench, 2026-09-04): the driver door panel's damaged screw hole carried the same metal-washer stopgap as the passenger side, in the same position, but bonded with J-B WELD (two-part epoxy) rather than the passenger side's cyanoacrylate. It sits directly under the DPPRK87 bracket footprint, so it had to come off or go flush. Paul tried the passenger-side technique first — grip with pliers, twist in plane — under an agreed stop rule (hand effort only, stop at the first sign of plastic moving or whitening). IT TWISTED RIGHT OFF.\n\nTHE PREDICTION THAT WAS WRONG: this session argued at length that the twist would probably NOT transfer from CA to epoxy — that JB Weld is tough rather than brittle, that on scuffed ABS the epoxy-plastic bond typically exceeds the ABS surface layer's own cohesive strength, and that the likely failure mode was tearing a disc of plastic out of an already-torn screw hole. The recommendation was to skip the twist and go straight to the Dremel. That reasoning was sound for a PREPPED bond and wrong for the bond that was actually there.\n\n⭐ THE CORRECTED RULE — the governing variable is SURFACE PREP, not adhesive chemistry. Two stopgap washers, two different adhesives, same result: both sheared at the glue line under pliers, neither took plastic. Both were laid on a dirty, unscuffed, decades-of-Armor-All 37-year-old panel back. A contaminated bond fails at the interface no matter which adhesive it is, and lands far below that adhesive's spec strength. The old STEP 7 framing ('CA is brittle and weak in shear, that is why the twist works') generalized from one data point to a chemistry explanation, when the real explanation was the surface underneath.\n\nSCOPE — the corrected rule does NOT license twisting off any bonded part on this truck. It applies to UNPREPPED STOPGAP BONDS only. A bond laid the way this guide prescribes (scuff, degrease with 91%+ IPA, bond) should be assumed unremovable, and that asymmetry is worth stating whenever a future removal comes up.\n\nTWO FORWARD CONSEQUENCES, both still OPEN:\n1. REASSURING FOR THE BRACKET INSTALL. The easy release is evidence about PREP, not evidence that epoxy is weak here. A scuffed, degreased DPPRK87 bond will be substantially stronger than either stopgap was. Do not read 'the washer came off easily' as a reason to distrust the bracket bond.\n2. THE FLATNESS REQUIREMENT SURVIVES THE REMOVAL. There is almost certainly a residual cured-epoxy footprint on the panel where the washer was, and the DPPRK87 bracket lands on exactly that spot. CA gel has essentially no gap fill (already recorded in STEP 7), so the residual ring must be sanded FLAT — 120 grit on a RIGID BLOCK, verified by test-fitting the bracket and looking for light under it, not by feel. NOT YET DONE, and not yet inspected: nobody has confirmed whether the epoxy came away with the washer or stayed on the panel.\n\nALSO CARRIED, NOT YET ADJUDICATED BY PAUL — the adhesive choice for the bracket at THIS hole. This session recommended bonding the DPPRK87 bracket with J-B Weld Original (on shelf, TOOLS.md, 2025-10-17) INSTEAD OF the CA gel the kit ships, tacking with the gel for position if useful. Three reasons: (a) the bond site is a mixed surface of original ABS plus cured epoxy plus, formerly, steel — not bare ABS, so the vendor's implicit ABS-takes-CA-well argument is weaker here; (b) epoxy has gap fill where CA gel has none, which tolerates the imperfect plane a sanded residue will leave; (c) it removes the CA gel's ~180F ceiling against a 140-180F paint bake, which is still an OPEN QUESTION FOR LARRY in STEP 7 item 5. Paul has not ruled on this.\n\nMETHOD NOTE WORTH KEEPING: the sequencing was right even though the prediction was wrong. The cheap reversible attempt was taken FIRST, with an explicit stop rule written down before it started, and the expensive irreversible path (Dremel) held in reserve. A hard stop rule is what made a low-probability attempt cheap enough to be worth thirty seconds. That pattern generalizes past this panel.\n\nHEAT WAS AND REMAINS OUT, unaffected by this result: the washer is a thermal bridge sitting directly on the ABS, so there is no path that heats the glue without heating the plastic; and J-B Weld does not release until well above the ~221F where ABS softens. Warming lowers BOTH the epoxy's strength and the ABS's, and the ABS was already the weaker link. Recorded so it is not re-litigated."}
